      "title": "Fixed does not make every future rate identical",
      "narration": "A traditional fixed annuity is supported by the insurer's general account. The insurer bears the investment risk behind its contractual guarantees. Suppose Rosa's contract guarantees at least two percent interest but currently credits four percent for the stated period. Four percent is not automatically promised forever. A later declared rate can change within the contract's limits while the minimum guarantee remains applicable. Some designs guarantee a stated rate for several years; read that period. A fixed income option can provide level dollar payments, but level dollars do not guarantee level purchasing power. Inflation can reduce what the same monthly amount buys.",
